17592186045417] [17592186362390 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following set of functions:\n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(t)=t,\\,{f}_{2}(t)={t}^{2},\\,{f}_{3}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space $C[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ of continuous functions over $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨f(t),g(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **II.** ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- **III.** $S={{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t),{f}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product\n\n*Hint: $⟨{f}_{2},{f}_{3}⟩=0.$*\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186362390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "06ea44bd-2468-51be-aabc-e31db5c657e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362390 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is called orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t,{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\cdot {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{4}-(\\frac{(-\\pi )^{4}}{4}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥{f}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{1}(t),{f}_{3}(t)⟩=⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }{u}^{′}v\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-(-(-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\pi +\\pi +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =2\\pi \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${f}_{1}(t)⊥̸{f}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363144 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186363147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363142 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363142 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363142 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186363146 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363142 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "862dc94f-3b95-5dca-bd86-c0c2d3c02b0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363143 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186363143 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363143 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31df6980-e28f-5655-96b0-97100c221864" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186363144 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186363144 #edb/re